      "id": "https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/news/cyber-chief-uk-facing-ai-powered-attacks-on-critical-infrastructure-in-two-years",
      "url": "https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/news/cyber-chief-uk-facing-ai-powered-attacks-on-critical-infrastructure-in-two-years",
      "title": "UK cyber agency warns AI-powered ransomware could hit critical infrastructure by 2028",
      "content_html": "The UK’s National Cyber Security Centre (NCSC), the government’s cyber defense agency, said Tuesday that an AI-powered ransomware attack on Britain’s critical infrastructure is highly likely by 2028. Chief Executive Richard Horne warned that artificial intelligence is expected to help hostile actors find and exploit long-standing weaknesses faster and on a greater scale, rather than develop entirely new attack methods. Energy, transport, health and food systems are among the essential services at risk.\n\nThe NCSC also warned of a likely rise in cyber incidents in the coming months, citing underinvestment, outdated systems and known vulnerabilities that organizations have not patched. It assessed China as the most sophisticated and sustained cyber challenge facing the UK and said Russia and China are likely to use AI routinely for geopolitical aims by 2028. It also described Russian attempts to breach British military networks and Iranian cyber operations targeting the UK.\n\nThe government has announced £60 million for the first phase of a national Cyber Shield and £70 million for a National Exposure and Real-Time Vulnerability Centre to help identify weaknesses across critical infrastructure and the economy. In its annual report, the NCSC is urging organizations to invest in resilience and basic protections.",
      "date_published": "2026-11-03T12:00:00+00:00",
