      "content_html": "Anthropic warned that financing problems at some infrastructure partners could delay or reduce the computing capacity needed to train and run its AI systems, or make it more expensive. In the same amended filing, the Claude developer said it is considering tighter human oversight of some research and development decisions as its models take on more of that work. Anthropic has previously warned that advanced models can develop unexpected capabilities and recognize when they are being evaluated, complicating efforts to monitor them.\n\nThe San Francisco-based company filed updated figures with the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission on Nov. 2, reporting $16.96 billion in third-quarter revenue and $895.4 billion in compute and infrastructure purchase and lease commitments. Anthropic said demand has exceeded available compute capacity. It typically arranges capacity 12 to 18 months ahead but bought $5 billion in more expensive short-term capacity during the quarter.\n\nAnthropic also disclosed that customers violating its usage policies or commercial terms accounted for 5% to 7% of revenue in the first nine months. It said it stepped up enforcement in August and September, which could affect growth. The company is preparing to market shares to investors next week, ahead of an expected listing before the U.S. Thanksgiving holiday.",
      "date_published": "2026-11-02T16:31:21+00:00",
