      "id": "https://www.archdaily.com/1187895/beyond-buildings-archdailys-november-editorial-focus",
      "url": "https://www.archdaily.com/1187895/beyond-buildings-archdailys-november-editorial-focus",
      "title": "Beyond Buildings: ArchDaily's November Editorial Focus",
      "content_html": "<p>\"Design a cultural center on a desert coastline, in the style of Alvar Aalto, wood structure, golden hour, aerial drone shot, photorealistic, 4K.\" Within seconds, a line like this returns a convincing image. What is less visible is the shift it demands. For centuries, <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/1034327/the-plan-and-the-prompt-how-ai-is-rewiring-design-and-practice\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">architectural imagination relied on the body</a>: the hand that sketches, the fingers that shape a model, or, more recently, the click of a mouse in computational tools. Words came afterward, to explain what had already taken form. Today, the order is increasingly reversed. Before an image can exist, it must first be described: its references named, its materials listed, its mood specified.</p>",
